"[2511.09003v1] Detecting Emotional Dynamic Trajectories: An Evaluation Framework for Emotional Support in Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09003v1)\nciteturn5931view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09003v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Detecting Emotional Dynamic Trajectories: An Evaluation Framework for Emotional Support in Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Zhouxing Tan , cite6†Ruochong Xiong , cite7†Yulong Wan , cite8†Jinlong Ma , cite9†Hanlin Xue , cite10†Qichun Deng , cite11†Haifeng Jing , cite12†Zhengtong Zhang , cite13†Depei Liu , cite14†Shiyuan Luo , cite15†Junfei Liu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Emotional Dynamic Trajectories: An Evaluation Framework for Emotional Support in Language Models, by Zhouxing Tan and 10 other authors\nL14: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Emotional support is a core capability in human-AI interaction, with applications including psychological counseling, role play, and companionship. However, existing evaluations of large language models (LLMs) often rely on short, static dialogues and fail to capture the dynamic and long-term nature of emotional support.\nL16: To overcome this limitation, we shift from snapshot-based evaluation to trajectory-based assessment, adopting a user-centered perspective that evaluates models based on their ability to improve and stabilize user emotional states over time. Our framework constructs a large-scale benchmark consisting of 328 emotional contexts and 1,152 disturbance events, simulating realistic emotional shifts under evolving dialogue scenarios.\nL17: To encourage psychologically grounded responses, we constrain model outputs using validated emotion regulation strategies such as situation selection and cognitive reappraisal. User emotional trajectories are modeled as a first-order Markov process, and we apply causally-adjusted emotion estimation to obtain unbiased emotional state tracking.\nL18: Based on this framework, we introduce three trajectory-level metrics: Baseline Emotional Level (BEL), Emotional Trajectory Volatility (ETV), and Emotional Centroid Position (ECP). These metrics collectively capture user emotional dynamics over time and support comprehensive evaluation of long-term emotional support performance of LLMs. Extensive evaluations across a diverse set of LLMs reveal significant disparities in emotional support capabilities and provide actionable insights for model development.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2511.09003 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2511.09003v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09003†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Zhouxing Tan [cite21†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 05:47:28 UTC (1,793 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Emotional Dynamic Trajectories: An Evaluation Framework for Emotional Support in Language Models, by Zhouxing Tan and 10 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite16†View PDF L35:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite22†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite23†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L44: \nL45: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: