nd Verification (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09008v1)\nciteturn5931view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09008v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 13 Jul 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:A Neurosymbolic Approach to Natural Language Formalization and Verification\nL10: Authors:cite6†Sam Bayless , cite7†Stefano Buliani , cite8†Darion Cassel , cite9†Byron Cook , cite10†Duncan Clough , cite11†Rémi Delmas , cite12†Nafi Diallo , cite13†Ferhat Erata , cite14†Nick Feng , cite15†Dimitra Giannakopoulou , cite16†Aman Goel , cite17†Aditya Gokhale , cite18†Joe Hendrix , cite19†Marc Hudak , cite20†Dejan Jovanović , cite21†Andrew M. Kent , cite22†Benjamin Kiesl-Reiter , cite23†Jeffrey J.\nL11: Kuna , cite24†Nadia Labai , cite25†Joseph Lilien , cite26†Divya Raghunathan , cite27†Zvonimir Rakamarić , cite28†Niloofar Razavi , cite29†Michael Tautschnig , cite30†Ali Torkamani , cite31†Nathaniel Weir , cite32†Michael W. Whalen , cite33†Jianan Yao L12: View a PDF of the paper titled A Neurosymbolic Approach to Natural Language Formalization and Verification, by Sam Bayless and 27 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite34†View PDF cite35†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Large Language Models perform well at natural language interpretation and reasoning, but their inherent stochasticity limits their adoption in regulated industries like finance and healthcare that operate under strict policies.\nL16: To address this limitation, we present a two-stage neurosymbolic framework that (1) uses LLMs with optional human guidance to formalize natural language policies, allowing fine-grained control of the formalization process, and (2) uses inference-time autoformalization to validate logical correctness of natural language statements against those policies.\nL17: When correctness is paramount, we perform multiple redundant formalization steps at inference time, cross checking the formalizations for semantic equivalence. Our benchmarks demonstrate that our approach exceeds 99% soundness, indicating a near-zero false positive rate in identifying logical validity. Our approach produces auditable logical artifacts that substantiate the verification outcomes and can be used to improve the original text.\nL18: Comments:  | 20 pages, 12 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite36†arXiv:2511.09008 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite37†arXiv:2511.09008v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite38†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09008†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Sam Bayless [cite39†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 06:00:37 UTC (615 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Mon, 13 Jul 2026 19:54:42 UTC (514 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled A Neurosymbolic Approach to Natural Language Formalization and Verification, by Sam Bayless and 27 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite34†View PDF L36:   * cite35†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite40†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite41†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite42†< prev |   cite43†next > L45: \nL46: cite44†new | cite45†recent | cite46†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite47†cs L51: cite48†cs.AI L52: cite49†cs.LG L53: 